1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:12,070
The problem with the building that had always
been there was that it was full.

2
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:18,534
Fifty-one staff in a laboratory designed for
thirty. Evelyn's archive work had spread

3
00:00:18,554 --> 00:00:24,906
across two floors and a corridor. Voss's vector
platform — four constructs, eleven weeks,

4
00:00:24,926 --> 00:00:31,206
exactly as he had said — needed a suite of its own,
and the regulatory group was working

5
00:00:31,226 --> 00:00:33,639
out of what had been a break room.

6
00:00:34,039 --> 00:00:40,327
Catherine Adeyemi, four weeks into the job, brought
him a plan for a leased facility in

7
00:00:40,347 --> 00:00:46,779
the New Castle corridor: nine months, twenty-two
million dollars, a construction schedule

8
00:00:46,799 --> 00:00:48,229
and a risk register.

9
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:48,892
No.

10
00:00:49,292 --> 00:00:53,936
It's the only available shell space of that size
within thirty miles. I have looked at

11
00:00:53,956 --> 00:00:54,423
fourteen.

12
00:00:54,823 --> 00:00:56,613
I don't want a shell. I want the block.

13
00:00:57,014 --> 00:01:03,029
He turned the window into a map with one gloved
finger: the four brick buildings along

14
00:01:03,049 --> 00:01:09,065
the riverfront south of their own — a dead
cold-storage warehouse, a machine shop that

15
00:01:09,085 --> 00:01:15,101
had folded in 2019, a two-storey office nobody had
leased in six years, and a lot with

16
00:01:15,121 --> 00:01:15,944
weeds in it.

17
00:01:16,463 --> 00:01:21,026
Those are four separate owners, two of them estates,
one of them in litigation. That is

18
00:01:21,046 --> 00:01:23,766
two years of conveyancing, if it
can be done at all.

19
00:01:24,165 --> 00:01:26,246
Then it will be a busy weekend.

20
00:01:27,278 --> 00:01:35,387
Facilities. Research Building, four storeys, fitted:
nine thousand. Logistics and Warehouse:

21
00:01:35,407 --> 00:01:42,898
six thousand. Data Hall, four hundred racks,
powered: seventeen thousand. Auditorium,

22
00:01:42,918 --> 00:01:51,204
five hundred seats: five thousand. Service road,
perimeter wall and gatehouse: three thousand.

23
00:01:51,224 --> 00:01:57,567
Campus integration — deeds, permits,
utilities, local record — included.

24
00:01:58,156 --> 00:02:04,121
Thirty-four thousand Points. He had thirty-four
thousand four hundred, because the quarter

25
00:02:04,141 --> 00:02:10,106
had been very good and because the System paid for
growth the way a furnace pays for coal.

26
00:02:10,627 --> 00:02:11,246
All of it.

27
00:02:11,645 --> 00:02:14,265
That will leave you four hundred Points.

28
00:02:14,665 --> 00:02:16,735
I am aware of what subtraction is.

29
00:02:17,135 --> 00:02:18,418
Confirm placement.

30
00:02:18,818 --> 00:02:23,981
The four parcels south of this building and the
vacant lot. Research on the corner, the

31
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:29,045
data hall behind it where nobody can see the
transformers, logistics against the rail

32
00:02:29,065 --> 00:02:31,070
spur, the auditorium on the water.

33
00:02:31,471 --> 00:02:38,455
The estate in litigation will require the record to
be adjusted in three counties and

34
00:02:38,475 --> 00:02:39,938
one probate court.

35
00:02:40,338 --> 00:02:43,685
Then adjust it in three counties and
one probate court.

36
00:02:44,305 --> 00:02:50,317
Confirmed. Placement will complete between
Friday 01:00 and Saturday 04:00.

37
00:02:50,717 --> 00:02:54,387
He stood at the window for a while
after the panel closed.

38
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:56,117
The other voice stirred.

39
00:02:56,638 --> 00:02:58,389
You have spent everything.

40
00:02:58,790 --> 00:02:59,165
Yes.

41
00:02:59,564 --> 00:03:06,258
Again. As you did in September. You are a man who
empties his pockets every time he is

42
00:03:06,278 --> 00:03:07,429
given anything.

43
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:12,062
I am a man who understands that Points are not
money. Money compounds if you leave it

44
00:03:12,082 --> 00:03:16,314
alone. Points do not. They are a voucher, and
vouchers expire the moment someone else

45
00:03:16,334 --> 00:03:18,165
builds the thing you were saving for.

46
00:03:18,566 --> 00:03:22,969
And if something is needed next week and
there is nothing left?

47
00:03:23,368 --> 00:03:28,011
Then I will be four hundred Points and five buildings
richer than I was, and I will earn

48
00:03:28,031 --> 00:03:32,515
more, because that is what the machine pays me for.
It does not pay me to be careful.

49
00:03:32,535 --> 00:03:33,310
Read the terms.

50
00:03:33,711 --> 00:03:39,749
On Friday night the riverfront south of Division
Seven was four derelict lots and a weed

51
00:03:39,769 --> 00:03:40,163
patch.

52
00:03:40,562 --> 00:03:42,942
On Saturday morning it was a campus.

53
00:03:43,342 --> 00:03:49,130
A four-storey research building in white stone that
matched the first, with its glass

54
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:55,144
already grimy at the corners the way glass gets. A
data hall with a transformer yard and

55
00:03:55,164 --> 00:04:01,363
a chain-link fence that had been repaired twice. A
logistics building with a loading apron,

56
00:04:01,383 --> 00:04:07,172
bay numbers painted on the concrete and a fork-lift
charging inside. An auditorium on

57
00:04:07,192 --> 00:04:13,049
the water with five hundred seats in dark red
fabric, three of them stained. A service

58
00:04:13,069 --> 00:04:18,926
road with a gatehouse, a striped barrier and a
logbook with eight months of entries in

59
00:04:18,946 --> 00:04:20,362
four different hands.

60
00:04:20,762 --> 00:04:25,701
And on every door, in crimson, six curved
blades folded into a circle.

61
00:04:26,101 --> 00:04:31,876
The narrator will now tell you what this actually
looked like from the inside of a normal

62
00:04:31,896 --> 00:04:37,540
human life, because this is the part of the story
that people always skip and it is the

63
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:38,711
part that matters.

64
00:04:39,112 --> 00:04:45,980
On Monday morning, a planner in the City of Wilmington's
Department of Licenses and Inspections

65
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,578
opened her file on the riverfront overlay district,
because a colleague had asked a routine

66
00:04:52,598 --> 00:04:59,031
question about parking ratios. In the file were the
permits for the Umbrella campus: four

67
00:04:59,051 --> 00:05:05,484
buildings, phased between 2004 and 2011, signed off
by her predecessor and, in two cases,

68
00:05:05,504 --> 00:05:05,992
by her.

69
00:05:06,391 --> 00:05:12,270
She recognised her own handwriting. She recognised
her own initials. She could remember

70
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:16,202
the argument about the auditorium's
height, and losing it.

71
00:05:16,601 --> 00:05:22,142
She sat and looked at that for about forty seconds.
Then she closed the file, and finished

72
00:05:22,162 --> 00:05:27,393
her coffee, and answered the question about parking
ratios, because there was nothing

73
00:05:27,413 --> 00:05:33,016
in the file that was wrong, and because the
alternative to believing the file was believing

74
00:05:33,036 --> 00:05:37,032
something that no one in the world would
have believed her about.

75
00:05:37,432 --> 00:05:43,570
This is how it went, every time, for sixty-five
years. Not one person was fooled by an

76
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:49,658
argument. They were simply handed an unbroken
record, and left alone with it, and the

77
00:05:49,678 --> 00:05:52,881
human mind does the rest
without being asked.

78
00:05:53,281 --> 00:05:55,872
There was one exception, and he
was nineteen.

79
00:05:56,271 --> 00:06:01,827
A hobbyist called Marcus Reese had been flying a
drone along the Christina for two years

80
00:06:01,847 --> 00:06:07,276
and had eleven hundred photographs of that stretch
of water. On the Sunday he went out

81
00:06:07,296 --> 00:06:12,661
to shoot the tide and came home and looked at his
own archive, and the buildings were

82
00:06:12,681 --> 00:06:18,173
in it. In shots from 2024. In shots from the summer
before that. In a photograph he had

83
00:06:18,193 --> 00:06:20,771
taken on his birthday with his own hands.

84
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:26,610
He posted about it. Eight people replied. Three
explained he had mislabelled his folders,

85
00:06:26,630 --> 00:06:31,824
four made jokes, and one — an account with a
cartoon frog for a picture — told him it

86
00:06:31,844 --> 00:06:37,222
was happening everywhere and had been for years, and
sent him a forum link, and that was

87
00:06:37,242 --> 00:06:42,436
worse than being laughed at, because that was the
moment Marcus Reese understood that

88
00:06:42,456 --> 00:06:47,834
being right and being believed were going to be two
different things for the rest of his

89
00:06:47,854 --> 00:06:48,141
life.

90
00:06:48,540 --> 00:06:54,947
He went quiet for four years. He comes back in 2031,
and by then he is very good at this,

91
00:06:54,967 --> 00:06:57,691
and by then it does not matter at all.

92
00:06:58,091 --> 00:07:04,337
On Monday morning, four hundred and six people —
everyone from Division Seven and everyone

93
00:07:04,357 --> 00:07:10,811
from the Newark plant, bussed down at company expense —
sat in a five-hundred-seat auditorium

94
00:07:10,831 --> 00:07:16,521
that had not existed on Friday, and waited for the
Director to explain the campus.

95
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:22,990
Wesker stood backstage in the dark with his hands
behind his back, and did something he

96
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:27,750
had not done since the volcano, and would
not do again for a decade.

97
00:07:28,151 --> 00:07:29,381
He asked for help.

98
00:07:29,901 --> 00:07:31,019
Say something useful.

99
00:07:31,418 --> 00:07:34,800
You will walk out there and tell
them the facts.

100
00:07:35,199 --> 00:07:40,036
I will tell them the truth, which is that we are the
fastest-growing diagnostics company

101
00:07:40,056 --> 00:07:43,458
in North America and that there is a
great deal of work to do.

102
00:07:43,858 --> 00:07:49,437
And they will clap, and they will go back to their
benches, and by Thursday it will be

103
00:07:49,457 --> 00:07:54,516
as though you had sent an email. Listen to me.
What do they feel this morning?

104
00:07:54,918 --> 00:08:00,364
Frightened. The plant workers think the Wilmington
staff look down on them. The Wilmington

105
00:08:00,384 --> 00:08:05,770
staff think the plant means the science is over.
Everyone in that room saw four buildings

106
00:08:05,790 --> 00:08:09,293
appear over a weekend and has decided
not to ask about it.

107
00:08:09,694 --> 00:08:11,669
Then do not reassure them.

108
00:08:12,069 --> 00:08:12,664
Go on.

109
00:08:13,064 --> 00:08:19,030
Reassurance is what a manager gives. Give them the
fear itself, out loud, in your own

110
00:08:19,050 --> 00:08:25,721
mouth, before any of them dares say it. A crowd will
forgive you for anything except pretending

111
00:08:25,741 --> 00:08:31,918
that the thing in the room is not in the room. Name
it. Then stand still and say nothing

112
00:08:31,938 --> 00:08:38,187
for as long as you can bear it. Then give them
something enormous to be afraid of instead

113
00:08:38,207 --> 00:08:40,652
— something that is not themselves.

114
00:08:41,052 --> 00:08:41,972
A pause.

115
00:08:42,491 --> 00:08:43,586
And the pause is how long?

116
00:08:43,986 --> 00:08:47,562
Until it hurts. Then two seconds more.

117
00:08:47,962 --> 00:08:53,952
He walked out. No slides. No notes. No introduction
— he had told Catherine to cut it.

118
00:08:54,472 --> 00:08:57,122
There are four buildings outside that
were not there on Friday.

119
00:08:58,293 --> 00:09:00,983
Four hundred and six people
stopped moving.

120
00:09:01,503 --> 00:09:06,001
Nobody in this room is going to ask me how. I know
that, because it is Monday and not

121
00:09:06,021 --> 00:09:10,626
one of you has asked. You have all decided,
privately, to be adults about it, and to be

122
00:09:10,646 --> 00:09:14,242
grateful, and to go back to work. I want
you to know that I noticed.

123
00:09:14,961 --> 00:09:18,576
I am not going to explain it to you today. I am
going to tell you what it is for.

124
00:09:18,975 --> 00:09:21,216
And then he gave them the enormous thing.

125
00:09:21,735 --> 00:09:26,607
Four hundred million people on this planet will be
infected this year with something that

126
00:09:26,627 --> 00:09:31,664
a nineteen-dollar test could have caught in nineteen
minutes. Roughly one and a half million

127
00:09:31,684 --> 00:09:36,830
of them will die of it — not of anything exotic, not of
anything incurable, but of a diagnosis

128
00:09:36,850 --> 00:09:41,502
that arrived three days late. Every one of those
deaths is an *administrative* death.

129
00:09:41,522 --> 00:09:44,855
Somebody, somewhere, decided that the
test was too expensive.

130
00:09:45,134 --> 00:09:49,767
I have made it thirty-one dollars. I will make it
fourteen. I intend to make it free,

131
00:09:49,787 --> 00:09:54,419
everywhere, and I intend for that to be the least
interesting thing this company ever

132
00:09:54,439 --> 00:09:54,693
does.

133
00:09:54,972 --> 00:09:59,156
You are not going to be thanked for it. There will
be no parade. In ten years, people

134
00:09:59,176 --> 00:10:03,359
will use what you built here the way they use tap
water, and they will complain about

135
00:10:03,379 --> 00:10:05,239
it, and they will not know your names.

136
00:10:05,850 --> 00:10:10,301
I will know your names. I know all of them now. That
is the arrangement I am offering,

137
00:10:10,321 --> 00:10:13,109
and it is the only one I have ever
found worth making.

138
00:10:13,509 --> 00:10:18,827
He stopped talking. He did not say thank you, and he
did not wait to be applauded, and

139
00:10:18,847 --> 00:10:22,739
he was three steps towards the wing
before the room came apart.

140
00:10:23,139 --> 00:10:28,554
They stood. All of them, in about a second and a
half, including three hundred and eleven

141
00:10:28,574 --> 00:10:33,868
people who had been alive for nine weeks and
fifty-one who had not, and a British chief

142
00:10:33,888 --> 00:10:39,425
financial officer at the back who found that she had
got to her feet without having decided

143
00:10:39,445 --> 00:10:39,609
to.

144
00:10:40,759 --> 00:10:49,456
Devotion. Four hundred and six personnel addressed.
Morale: transformed. Thirty-four thousand

145
00:10:49,476 --> 00:10:57,799
four hundred Points. Level ten. Note: the passenger's
craft is operationally significant.

146
00:10:57,819 --> 00:11:00,049
Recommend continued use.

147
00:11:00,639 --> 00:11:07,074
In the wing, in the dark, Albert Wesker stood with
his back against a wall and read that

148
00:11:07,094 --> 00:11:10,448
panel twice, and what he felt
was not triumph.

149
00:11:10,969 --> 00:11:12,632
*Recommend continued use.*

150
00:11:13,033 --> 00:11:14,162
You are welcome.

151
00:11:14,562 --> 00:11:16,516
I had them in twenty-one sentences.

152
00:11:16,916 --> 00:11:22,495
You had them in the *pause*. You would have talked
through it. You have talked through

153
00:11:22,515 --> 00:11:28,421
every silence you have ever been given, because
silence is the one room you cannot control.

154
00:11:29,096 --> 00:11:29,471
Noted.

155
00:11:29,870 --> 00:11:34,958
And the narrator would like to point out, because
nobody in that building could have,

156
00:11:34,978 --> 00:11:40,306
that this was the single most dangerous minute in the
first year of Umbrella's existence.

157
00:11:40,326 --> 00:11:45,654
Not the Tyrant. Not the plant. This: the moment the
man who could not be moved discovered

158
00:11:45,674 --> 00:11:50,822
that the passenger in his skull was carrying the one
tool he did not have, and decided

159
00:11:50,842 --> 00:11:55,990
— coldly, without pleasure, the way he decided
everything — that he was going to start

160
00:11:56,010 --> 00:11:56,531
using it.

161
00:11:56,931 --> 00:12:01,062
Catherine found him on the roof that night
with a drawing of the campus.

162
00:12:01,581 --> 00:12:03,028
Four hundred Points.

163
00:12:03,427 --> 00:12:04,418
I beg your pardon?

164
00:12:04,818 --> 00:12:09,553
That's what you said to Pell on the phone. "Four hundred
left." He assumed you meant thousand.

165
00:12:09,573 --> 00:12:10,665
I don't think you did.

166
00:12:11,065 --> 00:12:16,095
Wesker looked out at five buildings that had
cost him everything he had.

167
00:12:16,615 --> 00:12:17,524
No. I did not.

168
00:12:17,923 --> 00:12:22,311
I've stopped asking where the money comes from. I
want you to know that's a decision I

169
00:12:22,331 --> 00:12:24,207
made, not a thing I failed to notice.

170
00:12:24,607 --> 00:12:25,040
I know.

171
00:12:25,441 --> 00:12:27,661
I want to know what you're going
to do with it.

172
00:12:28,061 --> 00:12:34,007
And he told her — not all of it, not the volcano or
the merge or the panel that hung in

173
00:12:34,027 --> 00:12:39,836
the air where nobody else could see it, but the
shape of the next ten years, in about

174
00:12:39,856 --> 00:12:44,774
ninety seconds, in the flat tone of a man
reading out a train timetable.

175
00:12:45,173 --> 00:12:51,168
When he had finished, Catherine Adeyemi stood on the
roof of a building that had appeared

176
00:12:51,188 --> 00:12:56,980
out of nothing, and heard a man describe a company
larger than any state on earth, and

177
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:02,725
believed every word, and understood in that moment
that she had already chosen, weeks

178
00:13:02,745 --> 00:13:05,564
ago, in a boardroom, over a cup of coffee.

179
00:13:06,083 --> 00:13:07,279
That's monstrous.

180
00:13:07,679 --> 00:13:08,866
Yes. Is it wrong?

181
00:13:09,268 --> 00:13:13,718
She thought about it for a long time. That is
the detail. She thought about it.

182
00:13:14,238 --> 00:13:15,828
Ask me in five years.

183
00:13:16,227 --> 00:13:17,101
I intend to.
